2026-02-11 02:25:39,090 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document Theta: Understanding Time Decay

Theta ($\Theta$) represents the rate of change between the option price and time, or time sensitivity—sometimes known as an option’s time decay. Theta indicates the amount an option’s price would decrease as the time to expiration decreases, all else being equal. For example, assume an investor is long an option with a theta of -0.50. The option’s price would decrease by 50 cents every day that passes, all else being equal.

Theta increases when options are at the money, and decreases when options are in and out of the money. As expiration nears, options decay faster. Long calls and puts typically have negative theta, while short ones have positive theta. By comparison, an instrument whose value is not eroded by time, such as a stock, would have zero theta. is relevant
