Theta is generally expressed as a negative number for long positions and a positive number for short positions. It is the daily amount by which an option's value is declining. For instance, a theta of -0.05 indicates that the option's price will decrease by five cents per day.

Options give the buyer the right to buy or sell an underlying asset at a predetermined price before the option expires. The strike price, also called the exercise price, is set when the contract is written. It is the price the underlying asset must reach before the option can be exercised.

Options Greeks, or simply “Greeks,” are invaluable pieces of information for options investors. The mathematical variables help investors calculate the risks that influence an option’s price, especially as it nears its expiration. is relevant
