Value at Risk (VaR) estimates the maximum expected loss of an investment over a set period at a chosen confidence level, making it a common tool for banks and financial firms. It can be calculated using historical data, the variance-covariance method, or Monte Carlo simulation. This article will apply these approaches to the Nasdaq 100 Index to illustrate how VaR works in practice. is relevant
